The refactor is complete and both gates pass.

**Summary**: I made the binlog module idiomatic Rust by (1) renaming the snake_case `log_event_type` enum to `LogEventType` with PascalCase variants (`PduSent`, `PacketRecv`, etc.) and dropping the `#[allow(non_camel_case_types)]` lint suppression, (2) converting the nine `Cnx`-first free functions (`binlog_dropped_packet`, `binlog_buffered_packet`, `binlog_outgoing_packet`, `binlog_packet_lost`, `binlog_negotiated_alpn`, `binlog_transport_extension`, `binlog_new_connection`, `binlog_close_connection`, `binlog_cc_dump`) into inherent methods on `Cnx` — keeping the `binlog_` prefix to namespace them apart from the `log_*` unified-log dispatchers on the same type, (3) converting `set_binlog`/`enable_binlog` into methods on `Quic` (matching the established pattern in `qlog.rs` and `logger.rs`), (4) shedding the redundant `binlog_` prefix on the three file-only writers so call sites read `binlog::pdu(...)` / `binlog::packet(...)` / `binlog::tls_ticket(...)`, and (5) refreshing the module docstring and removing a stale clippy comment that no longer reflected the code (the crate-wide `#![allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]` in `lib.rs` already covers the long signatures). No callers needed updating — the binlog symbols aren't yet referenced by sibling modules.


